{"theorems":[{"slogan_id":9584048,"theorem_id":24648249,"name":"Lemma 3.2 (The Nash-Williams inequality and recurrence criterion)","body":" For any distinct vertices $a$ and $z$ separated by pairwise disjoint cutsets $\\Pi _1,\\, \\cdots ,\\, \\Pi _{n}$ in a finite network, \\[ \\mathscr {R}(a\\leftrightarrow z)\\geq \\sum \\limits _{k=1}^{n} \\left (\\sum \\limits _{e\\in \\Pi _k}\\mathbf {c}(e)\\right )^{-1}. \\] For any sequence $\\left \\{\\Pi _n\\right \\}_n$ of pairwise disjoint finite cutsets in an infinite locally finite network $G$ such that each $\\Pi _n$ separates $a$ from $\\infty ,$ \\[ \\mathscr {R}(a\\leftrightarrow \\infty )\\geq \\sum \\limits _{n}^{\\infty } \\left (\\sum \\limits _{e\\in \\Pi _n}\\mathbf {c}(e)\\right )^{-1}; \\] and particularly $G$ is recurrent when the right-hand side is $\\infty .$","slogan":"The effective resistance between two distinct vertices is at least the sum of the reciprocals of the total conductances of pairwise disjoint cutsets separating them. In an infinite network, the effective resistance from a vertex to infinity is at least the infinite sum of the reciprocals of the total conductances of pairwise disjoint finite cutsets separating that vertex from infinity. If this infinite sum diverges, the network is recurrent. This holds for locally finite networks.","theorem_type":"lemma","label":null,"link":null,"paper":{"paper_id":"2010.01530v1","source":"arXiv","title":"Phase transition of disordered random networks on quasi-transitive graphs","authors":["Yuelin Liu","Kainan Xiang"],"link":"http://arxiv.org/abs/2010.01530v1","summary":null,"journal_ref":null,"primary_category":"math.PR","categories":["math.PR"],"citations":0,"year":2020,"journal_published":false},"similarity":0.7007273792353467,"score":0.7007273792353467,"has_metadata":true},{"slogan_id":5255460,"theorem_id":20314933,"name":"Corollary 6.3","body":"Let $G$ be a transient, connected graph. Then,\\begin{equation*}  R^F(x,y) = \\frac{1}{c_x \\cdot \\mathbb {P}_x[\\tau _y < \\tau _x^+]} \\end{equation*}holds for all $x,y \\in V$ if and only if $G$ is a subgraph of an infinite line.","slogan":"For a transient connected graph, the formula for the effective resistance between two nodes holds for all pairs if and only if the graph is a subgraph of an infinite line. The formula involves the reciprocal of the product of the conductance at the starting node and the probability of hitting the target before returning to the start. This equivalence characterizes graphs that are essentially one-dimensional.","theorem_type":"corollary","label":null,"link":null,"paper":{"paper_id":"1902.01110v2","source":"arXiv","title":"On the Probabilistic Representation of the Free Effective Resistance of Infinite Graphs","authors":["Tobias Weihrauch","Stefan Bachmann"],"link":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1902.01110v2","summary":null,"journal_ref":null,"primary_category":"math.PR","categories":["math.PR"],"citations":0,"year":2019,"journal_published":false},"similarity":0.6605391513337234,"score":0.6605391513337234,"has_metadata":true}]}